Two of the vertices of a regular octahedron are to be chosen at random. What is the probability that they will be the endpoints of an edge of the octahedron? Express your answer as a common fraction. [asy]
size(150);
pair A, B, C, D, E, F;
A=(1,1);
B=(-1,-1);
C=(0,6);
D=(0,-6);
E=(6, 0);
F=(-6,0);
draw(C--F--D--E--C--B--F);
draw(D--B--E);
draw(F--A--C, dashed);
draw(D--A--E, dashed);
[/asy]

A square is divided into five congruent rectangles, as shown. If the perimeter of each of these five rectangles is 36 inches, what is the perimeter of the square, in inches?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(0,5)--(5,5)--(5,0)--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(1,5));
draw((2,0)--(2,5));
draw((3,0)--(3,5));
draw((4,0)--(4,5));
[/asy]

results.ground_truth_output:
The common ratio is $\frac19 \div \frac13 = \frac13$.
The sum of the first five terms is $\frac{\frac13(1-\left(\frac13\right)^5)}{1-\frac13} = \boxed{\frac{121}{243}}$.
The answer is: \frac{121}{243}
